course: dictionary
lesson: 7603
title: Principles: the rules we live by
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A principle is a basic rule or belief about what is right, and it guides how you behave. «She has strong principles, so she never tells a lie.» Her principles are her firm rules about honesty, and she keeps to them.
explain: A principle can also be a basic law or fact that explains how something works. In science you learn the principle that hot air rises, and in maths you learn principles such as «what you do to one side you do to the other».
explain: Two useful phrases are «on principle» and «in principle». «On principle» means because you believe it is right. «In principle» means in general or in theory, but maybe not in practice. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
fact: A principle is a basic rule or belief about what is right, or a basic law or fact about how something works.
fact: «On principle» means because of what you believe is right.
fact: «In principle» means in general or in theory, but maybe not in practice.
fact: A principle is a general rule used in reasoning, such as «do not harm the innocent».
fact: Grown-ups call a rule like «3 socks are always enough to be sure of a matching pair» the pigeonhole principle.
exercise: «She has strong principles, so she never tells a lie.» What does principles mean here? | Things she likes to eat for lunch | *Basic beliefs about what is right that guide how she behaves | Loud shouts when she is cross
exercise: In science, what is a principle? | A loud noise made by a machine | *A basic law or fact that explains how something works | A kind of picnic blanket | A type of school uniform
exercise: «He said no to the bribe on principle.» Why did he say no? | Because he was late for school | Because the bribe was too small | *Because he believes taking a bribe is wrong | Because he was hungry
test:
«In principle, the plan could work, but nobody has tried it yet.» What does in principle mean? | *In general or in theory, but maybe not in practice | Definitely and for certain, with no doubts | Without any rules at all | Only on a Tuesday
«With socks of only two colours, 3 socks are always enough to be sure of a matching pair.» Grown-ups call this the pigeonhole principle. What is a principle here? | A pot to keep socks in | *A general rule that always works, shown by this example | A kind of sock made from wool | A mistake in the sums
exam: Which sentence uses principle to mean a basic rule or belief? | «The principle of the lever helps us lift heavy stones.» | *«Her principles stop her from ever cheating in a game.» | «The principle of the bus timetable is on the wall.» | «The children had a principle picnic in the park.»
